# TaskFlow development rules

## Stack

- Use Next.js with the App Router.
- Use React, TypeScript and Tailwind CSS.
- Do not introduce a new library unless the user explicitly approves it.
- Use the existing project dependencies before suggesting new ones.

## TypeScript

- Do not use `any`.
- Prefer explicit types for component props and domain data.
- Reuse existing shared types when appropriate.
- Do not suppress TypeScript errors without explaining the reason.

## Next.js and React

- Keep components as Server Components by default.
- Add `"use client"` only when hooks, browser APIs or user interaction require it.
- Use `next/link` for internal route navigation.
- Use semantic HTML elements.
- Do not nest interactive elements such as a link inside a button.

## Components

- Reuse existing components before creating new ones.
- Keep each component focused on one clear responsibility.
- Preserve existing public component APIs unless the task requires changing them.
- When changing a shared component, inspect and update all affected usages.

## Styling

- Use Tailwind CSS.
- Follow a mobile-first approach.
- Preserve the current TaskFlow visual language.
- Prefer standard Tailwind values over arbitrary values.
- Maintain visible keyboard focus styles.

## Language

- Use English for code, variables, types, file names and commit messages.
- Use Portuguese for text visible in the user interface.

## Scope and safety

- Change only files related to the requested task.
- Do not edit generated files.
- Do not expose secrets, passwords or environment variables.
- Do not run destructive commands.
- Do not commit code automatically.
- Explain any unresolved error instead of hiding it.

## Verification

After an implementation:

1. Review every changed file.
2. Check TypeScript errors.
3. Run lint when available.
4. Test the affected behavior.
5. Report unresolved warnings or errors.